n  but normally we won't be able to do this  because our sensors are small  and we're not able to track a large  large area so we only have very local  information so  essential ideally we want to be able to  work even with short time horizons  but we can see the difference of our  trajectories vary with the time horizon  so  in this video what you can see is the  one on the  when you start using more information  you can get to the goal quicker  both trajectories are using the red lcs  because both of them  are moving with an r by q equals hundred  but the one on the right  is uh using the red lcs to move towards  the right  and then loop around a bit and then go  to left whereas this trajectory is going  straight  and it's able to use that bit of  information that it has to move straight  to this goal  and that's really interesting but now  what we want to do is we want to look at  if we sweep through all the time  horizons and all the ryq's  what are the best trajectories we can  produce and how do we look at that  so for that we have this plot  so on this plot what we're doing is on  the y-axis we're plotting  uh the energy spent across the entire  trajectory  and on the x-axis we're plotting what  the state error is along the entire  trajectory  and we get these nice pareto optimal  curves going around here  and what we see is the one the region  around one  is where we're using massive energy to  get to where we want to go  and in the region around two is where we  have this  optimal movement that we have to get  towards the goal but in a really energy  optimal way  and beyond that you get really bad  trajectories and these trajectories  even if they are technically using  lesser energy than  two the the trade-off in uh  uh the state error is way too high at  them so we're really interested in  these uh trajectories around two  also what uh thing we notice is that a  lot of the  uh time where i a lot of the time  horizons that are even really short  really do 